from (values
  (1, 'Diagnostic: where are you now?', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Rust achieves memory safety primarily through...","options":["a garbage collector that runs at intervals","a compile-time ownership and borrowing system","manual malloc and free calls","reference counting on every value"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Rust checks ownership and borrowing rules at compile time, so most memory bugs are caught before the program runs - see ''Why Rust''."},{"prompt":"In Rust, variables are by default...","options":["mutable","immutable","global","nullable"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A plain let binding is immutable; you write let mut to allow changes - see ''Variables and types''."},{"prompt":"When a value is moved to a new owner, the original binding...","options":["is copied","can no longer be used","becomes null","is shared by both"],"answer":1,"explanation":"For non-Copy types a move transfers ownership and invalidates the source - see ''Ownership''."},{"prompt":"How many mutable references to the same value may exist at once?","options":["exactly one","any number","two","zero"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The borrow checker allows only one mutable reference (or many shared ones) at a time - see ''Borrowing and references''."},{"prompt":"The idiomatic Rust type for a value that may be absent is...","options":["null","Option<T>","void","Empty"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Rust has no null; absence is modeled by Option<T> with Some and None - see ''Error handling''."},{"prompt":"The type used for an operation that may succeed or fail is...","options":["Result<T, E>","Try<T>","Maybe<T>","Status"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Result<T, E> carries Ok(value) or Err(error) - see ''Error handling''."},{"prompt":"A trait in Rust is closest to which concept?","options":["a class","a shared interface of behavior","a namespace","a macro"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A trait defines methods a type can implement, like an interface - see ''Generics and traits''."},{"prompt":"Calling .iter() on a vector gives you...","options":["a new vector","an iterator over its elements","the length","a slice"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Iterators produce elements lazily and pair with adapters like map and filter - see ''Collections and iterators''."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (2, 'Why Rust: safety and performance together', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain why Rust can be both memory-safe and as fast as C, and compile and run a first program. Warm-up: from the diagnostic, what does Rust use instead of a garbage collector to stay safe?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Most languages pick one side of a tradeoff. C and C++ give you full control and top speed but let you corrupt memory: use-after-free, double-free, data races. Java, Go, and Python add a garbage collector for safety but pay for it with pauses and overhead. Rust refuses the tradeoff. Its compiler enforces an ownership system that proves, at compile time, that memory is used correctly, so there is no garbage collector and no runtime penalty. The result is C-like performance with the safety guarantees of a managed language. Abstractions like iterators and closures are zero-cost: they compile to the same tight machine code you would write by hand."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"A minimal program:\n\n    fn main() {\n        println!(\"Hello, Rust!\");\n    }\n\nCompile and run with `cargo run`. Cargo is Rust''s build tool and package manager. Here is a function that returns a value; note the type after the arrow and the lack of a semicolon on the final expression, which makes it the return value:\n\n    fn square(n: i32) -> i32 {\n        n * n\n    }\n\n    fn main() {\n        let result = square(9);\n        println!(\"{}\", result); // prints 81\n    }"},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What runtime component does Rust avoid that Java and Go rely on? 2) In `fn square(n: i32) -> i32`, what does `i32` after the arrow mean? 3) Why does the last line of square have no semicolon? 4) Which command builds and runs a Cargo project? 5) What does ''zero-cost abstraction'' promise? Answers: 1) a garbage collector. 2) the function returns a 32-bit signed integer. 3) an expression without a trailing semicolon is the block''s return value; adding one would make it a statement returning nothing. 4) cargo run. 5) high-level constructs compile to code as fast as hand-written low-level code, with no runtime overhead."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: thinking safety must cost speed - Rust proves safety at compile time, so there is nothing to pay at runtime. Mistake: adding a semicolon after the final expression you meant to return, which turns it into the unit value (). Exit: (a) Does Rust use a garbage collector? (b) What makes an expression the return value of a function body? Answers: no; being the final expression with no trailing semicolon."}]}'::jsonb, '''Zero-cost abstraction'' means a high-level construct compiles down to code as fast as the hand-written low-level version - you pay no runtime tax for the convenience.', 12),
  (3, 'Variables, types, and mutability', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Declare immutable and mutable variables, read Rust''s core scalar and compound types, and use shadowing. Warm-up: is a plain `let x = 5;` mutable or immutable by default?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A binding made with `let` is immutable: you cannot reassign it. Write `let mut` when you truly need to change a value. This default pushes you toward code that is easy to reason about. Rust is statically typed, but the compiler infers most types, so you rarely annotate them. The scalar types are integers (i32, u32, i64, and so on), floating point (f64), the boolean bool, and char. Compound types include the tuple, which groups a fixed set of values of possibly different types, and the array, a fixed-length run of one type. Shadowing lets you reuse a name with a new `let`, which can even give it a new type."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Immutability and mut:\n\n    let x = 5;\n    // x = 6;      // compile error: cannot assign twice\n    let mut y = 5;\n    y = 6;          // fine, y is mutable\n\nAnnotations, a tuple, and an array:\n\n    let pi: f64 = 3.14159;\n    let point: (i32, i32) = (3, 4);\n    let (a, b) = point;      // destructure: a = 3, b = 4\n    let days = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];\n    println!(\"{}\", days[0]); // 1\n\nShadowing, changing the type under one name:\n\n    let spaces = \"   \";        // a string slice\n    let spaces = spaces.len(); // now a number, 3"},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Which keyword makes a binding changeable? 2) What is the type of `(true, ''z'', 7)`? 3) How do you read the second element of the tuple `let p = (10, 20);`? 4) Does shadowing require `mut`? 5) What is `[0; 3]`? Answers: 1) mut, as in let mut. 2) a tuple of (bool, char, i32). 3) p.1, which is 20. 4) no; shadowing uses a fresh let and can even change the type. 5) an array of three elements, each 0, that is [0, 0, 0]."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: forgetting `mut` and then trying to reassign, which the compiler rejects. Mistake: confusing shadowing (a new binding) with mutation (changing the existing value). Exit: (a) Are `let` bindings mutable by default? (b) How do you index the first element of an array named days? Answers: no, they are immutable; days[0]."}]}'::jsonb, 'let binds a name; let mut lets that binding change; shadowing with a fresh let can even change the type under the same name.', 12),
  (4, 'Ownership: Rust''s core idea', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"State the three ownership rules and predict when a value is moved, copied, or dropped. Warm-up: after you move a String to a new owner, can you still use the old binding?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Ownership is how Rust manages memory without a garbage collector. Three rules: each value has a single owner; there is exactly one owner at a time; when the owner goes out of scope, the value is dropped and its memory freed. For heap types like String, assigning to a new variable or passing to a function moves ownership rather than copying the data; the old binding becomes invalid, which prevents two owners from freeing the same memory. Small stack types like i32 and bool implement the Copy trait, so they are duplicated instead of moved, and the original stays usable. If you want to keep a heap value after handing it off, you can clone it explicitly."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"A move invalidates the source:\n\n    let s1 = String::from(\"hello\");\n    let s2 = s1;            // ownership moves to s2\n    // println!(\"{}\", s1);  // compile error: s1 was moved\n    println!(\"{}\", s2);     // ok\n\nCopy types are duplicated, not moved:\n\n    let a = 5;\n    let b = a;              // i32 is Copy\n    println!(\"{} {}\", a, b); // 5 5, both still valid\n\nPassing to a function moves ownership; here it is returned to keep using it:\n\n    fn take_and_give(s: String) -> String { s }\n    let s = String::from(\"hi\");\n    let s = take_and_give(s); // moved in, moved back out\n\nOr clone to keep your own copy:\n\n    let original = String::from(\"data\");\n    let copy = original.clone();\n    println!(\"{} {}\", original, copy); // both valid"},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) How many owners does a value have at a time? 2) After `let s2 = s1;` where s1 is a String, is s1 still usable? 3) Why can you keep using an i32 after assigning it elsewhere? 4) What happens to a value when its owner goes out of scope? 5) How do you deliberately make a second independent String? Answers: 1) exactly one. 2) no, it was moved into s2. 3) i32 implements Copy, so it is duplicated, not moved. 4) it is dropped and its memory is freed. 5) call .clone() on it."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: using a variable after it has been moved - the compiler flags ''value borrowed after move''. Mistake: reaching for .clone() everywhere out of habit, which copies data you may not need to copy. Exit: (a) What are the three ownership rules? (b) Which trait lets a value be copied instead of moved? Answers: one owner per value, one owner at a time, drop on scope exit; the Copy trait."}]}'::jsonb, 'Ownership rules: each value has exactly one owner; when the owner goes out of scope the value is dropped; assigning or passing a non-Copy value moves ownership.', 15),
  (5, 'Checkpoint 1', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"How does Rust free heap memory without a garbage collector?","options":["you call free() by hand","the value is dropped when its owner leaves scope","a background thread reclaims it","memory is never freed"],"answer":1,"explanation":"When the owning variable goes out of scope, Rust drops the value and frees its memory automatically."},{"prompt":"After `let s2 = s1;` where s1 is a String, using s1 again causes...","options":["a runtime panic","a compile error, because s1 was moved","a silent copy","undefined behavior"],"answer":1,"explanation":"String is not Copy, so the assignment moves ownership and the compiler rejects later use of s1."},{"prompt":"Which keyword is required to reassign a variable?","options":["var","mut","let again alone","const"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A binding must be declared with let mut before it can be reassigned."},{"prompt":"Why can an i32 be used after being assigned to another variable?","options":["integers are references","i32 implements Copy, so it is duplicated","the compiler makes an exception for math","it is stored on the heap"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Small scalar types implement Copy, so assignment duplicates the value rather than moving it."},{"prompt":"The final expression of a function body, written with no semicolon, is...","options":["ignored","the return value","a syntax error","always ()"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An expression without a trailing semicolon becomes the block''s return value."},{"prompt":"To keep an independent second copy of a String, you call...","options":[".copy()",".clone()",".dup()",".share()"],"answer":1,"explanation":"clone() performs a deep copy so both bindings own separate data."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (6, 'Borrowing and references', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Pass values by reference to avoid moving them, and apply the borrowing rules the compiler enforces. Warm-up: how many mutable references to one value may exist at the same time?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Moving a value every time you pass it around would be tedious. Instead you borrow it with a reference. A shared reference &T lets a function read a value without taking ownership; the owner keeps it. A mutable reference &mut T lets a function change the value. The borrow checker enforces two rules that prevent data races at compile time: at any moment you may have either any number of shared references or exactly one mutable reference, but not both; and every reference must be valid for as long as it is used, so no reference can outlive the value it points to. These rules stop two parts of the code from writing at once, or reading while another writes."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Borrow to read without moving:\n\n    fn length(s: &String) -> usize {\n        s.len()\n    }\n    let s = String::from(\"hello\");\n    let n = length(&s);   // s is borrowed, not moved\n    println!(\"{} {}\", s, n); // s still usable, prints hello 5\n\nBorrow mutably to change in place:\n\n    fn add_bang(s: &mut String) {\n        s.push(''!'');\n    }\n    let mut msg = String::from(\"hi\");\n    add_bang(&mut msg);\n    println!(\"{}\", msg); // hi!\n\nThe rule the checker enforces:\n\n    let mut v = String::from(\"x\");\n    let r1 = &v;          // shared borrow\n    let r2 = &v;          // another shared borrow, fine\n    // let r3 = &mut v;   // error: cannot borrow mutably while shared borrows are alive\n    println!(\"{} {}\", r1, r2);"},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What symbol creates a shared reference? 2) What does &mut allow that & does not? 3) Can you hold a &String and a &mut String to the same value simultaneously? 4) Does calling `length(&s)` move s? 5) Why must a reference not outlive its value? Answers: 1) the ampersand, &. 2) mutating the borrowed value in place. 3) no; a mutable borrow cannot coexist with any shared borrow. 4) no, it borrows s, which stays owned by the caller. 5) otherwise it would point at freed memory, a dangling reference the compiler forbids."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: trying to take a &mut reference while shared references are still in use, which the borrow checker rejects. Mistake: forgetting to write &mut both at the borrow site and in the parameter type. Exit: (a) How many mutable references may a value have at once? (b) What kind of reference do you pass when a function only needs to read? Answers: at most one; a shared reference, &T."}]}'::jsonb, '&T is a shared (read-only) reference; &mut T is an exclusive (writable) reference. You may have many &T or exactly one &mut T at a time, never both.', 14),
  (7, 'Slices: borrowing a piece', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Borrow part of a string or array with a slice, and prefer &str parameters for flexible functions. Warm-up: does taking a slice of a String copy its characters?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A slice is a reference to a contiguous run of elements rather than the whole collection. A string slice, written &str, borrows part of a String; an array slice &[T] borrows part of an array or vector. A slice stores just a pointer to the start and a length, so it is cheap and copies no data. Because it is a borrow, the same borrowing rules apply: the slice must not outlive the data it views. Writing functions that take &str instead of &String makes them work on both String values and string literals, which are themselves &str."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"String slices name ranges by byte index:\n\n    let s = String::from(\"hello world\");\n    let hello = &s[0..5];   // \"hello\"\n    let world = &s[6..11];  // \"world\"\n    println!(\"{} {}\", hello, world);\n\nA function that returns the first word as a slice:\n\n    fn first_word(s: &str) -> &str {\n        let bytes = s.as_bytes();\n        for (i, &b) in bytes.iter().enumerate() {\n            if b == b'' '' {\n                return &s[0..i];\n            }\n        }\n        s\n    }\n    println!(\"{}\", first_word(\"hello world\")); // hello\n\nArray slices work the same way:\n\n    let nums = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50];\n    let middle = &nums[1..4]; // [20, 30, 40]\n    println!(\"{}\", middle.len()); // 3"},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What two pieces of information does a slice store? 2) Write the slice expression for the first three bytes of a String s. 3) Why prefer &str over &String as a parameter type? 4) Does &nums[1..4] copy the array elements? 5) What is the length of &nums[1..4] when nums has 5 elements? Answers: 1) a pointer to the start and a length. 2) &s[0..3]. 3) it accepts both String values and string literals, since both coerce to &str. 4) no, it borrows a view into the existing array. 5) 3, covering indices 1, 2, and 3."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: slicing a string in the middle of a multi-byte character, which panics at runtime because ranges are byte indices. Mistake: returning a slice that borrows a local value, then letting the value drop - the borrow checker prevents the dangling reference. Exit: (a) What does &str borrow? (b) Does a slice own its data? Answers: a contiguous range of a string; no, it borrows a view and owns nothing."}]}'::jsonb, 'A slice like &s[0..5] borrows a contiguous range and stores a pointer plus a length; it owns nothing and cannot outlive the data it views.', 12),
  (8, 'Structs and enums', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Define structs with fields and methods, and model a fixed set of cases with enums that carry data. Warm-up: which construct would you use to represent a value that is exactly one of a small, fixed set of shapes?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A struct bundles related data under named fields into a single type, like a record. You attach behavior with an impl block, where methods take &self to read the instance or &mut self to change it. An enum defines a type whose value is exactly one of several named variants, and each variant can carry its own data. Enums shine for modeling states and shapes precisely: a value is one case or another, never an invalid mix. Together, structs and enums let you build a domain model the compiler can check for you."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"A struct with a method:\n\n    struct Rectangle {\n        width: u32,\n        height: u32,\n    }\n    impl Rectangle {\n        fn area(&self) -> u32 {\n            self.width * self.height\n        }\n    }\n    let r = Rectangle { width: 3, height: 4 };\n    println!(\"{}\", r.area()); // 12\n\nAn enum whose variants carry different data:\n\n    enum Shape {\n        Circle(f64),          // radius\n        Rectangle(f64, f64),  // width, height\n    }\n    fn area(shape: &Shape) -> f64 {\n        match shape {\n            Shape::Circle(r) => 3.14159 * r * r,\n            Shape::Rectangle(w, h) => w * h,\n        }\n    }\n    let c = Shape::Circle(2.0);\n    println!(\"{}\", area(&c)); // 12.56636"},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Which construct groups named fields into one type? 2) What does a method''s &self parameter give access to? 3) How do you let a method modify the instance? 4) Can an enum variant carry data? 5) In `Shape::Circle(2.0)`, what is 2.0? Answers: 1) a struct. 2) the instance the method is called on, read-only. 3) take &mut self instead of &self. 4) yes, each variant may hold its own values. 5) the data for that variant, here the circle''s radius."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: taking &self when you meant to mutate a field - the compiler will not let you assign through a shared borrow. Mistake: forgetting the type name prefix on a variant, writing Circle instead of Shape::Circle. Exit: (a) Where do you define a struct''s methods? (b) What is the defining feature of an enum? Answers: in an impl block for that struct; it is exactly one of a fixed set of named variants."}]}'::jsonb, 'struct groups named fields into one type; enum lists a fixed set of variants, each of which may carry its own data; impl blocks attach methods to either.', 14),
  (9, 'Checkpoint 2', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"To let a function read a String without taking ownership, you pass...","options":["the String by value","a shared reference &String or &str","a clone every time","a mutable reference"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A shared reference borrows the value so the owner keeps it and the function can read it."},{"prompt":"A string slice &str stores...","options":["a full copy of the characters","a pointer and a length","an index only","the whole String"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A slice is just a pointer to the start of a range plus its length; it copies nothing."},{"prompt":"Methods are attached to a struct inside...","options":["a trait block","an impl block","the struct''s field list","the main function"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An impl block for the type holds its methods, each taking self, &self, or &mut self."},{"prompt":"An enum is best suited to represent...","options":["a record of many fields at once","a value that is exactly one of a fixed set of cases","a growable list","a reference"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each enum value is one named variant, which models mutually exclusive cases precisely."},{"prompt":"Which borrow pattern does the checker reject?","options":["two shared references at once","one mutable reference alone","a shared reference plus a mutable reference at the same time","no references at all"],"answer":2,"explanation":"You may have many shared references or one mutable reference, but never both simultaneously."},{"prompt":"A method that changes a field of its instance must take...","options":["self","&self","&mut self","no self"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Mutating through the instance requires an exclusive &mut self receiver."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (10, 'Pattern matching with match', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Branch on the shape of a value with match, destructure data out of variants, and rely on exhaustiveness. Warm-up: what does the underscore arm _ do in a match?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"match compares a value against a series of patterns and runs the arm of the first one that fits. Its superpower is that it is exhaustive: the compiler refuses to build unless every possible case is handled, so you cannot forget a variant. Patterns can also destructure, pulling the data out of an enum variant or tuple and binding it to names you use in that arm. The wildcard pattern _ matches anything and serves as a default. For quickly handling just one case, `if let` is a compact shorthand for a match with a single pattern of interest."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Exhaustive match over an enum:\n\n    enum Coin { Penny, Nickel, Dime, Quarter }\n    fn value_in_cents(coin: &Coin) -> u32 {\n        match coin {\n            Coin::Penny => 1,\n            Coin::Nickel => 5,\n            Coin::Dime => 10,\n            Coin::Quarter => 25,\n        }\n    }\n\nDestructuring the data inside a variant:\n\n    let maybe = Some(7);\n    match maybe {\n        Some(n) => println!(\"got {}\", n), // binds n = 7\n        None => println!(\"nothing\"),\n    }\n\nA wildcard default and the if let shorthand:\n\n    let x = 3;\n    match x {\n        1 => println!(\"one\"),\n        2 => println!(\"two\"),\n        _ => println!(\"something else\"), // catch-all\n    }\n    if let Some(n) = Some(42) {\n        println!(\"value is {}\", n);       // runs only for Some\n    }"},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What does it mean for a match to be exhaustive? 2) What pattern acts as a catch-all default? 3) In `Some(n) => ...`, what does n bind to? 4) When is `if let` a good choice over match? 5) What happens if you omit a variant and there is no wildcard? Answers: 1) every possible value of the type is covered by some arm. 2) the underscore, _. 3) the value carried inside the Some variant. 4) when you only care about one pattern and want to ignore the rest. 5) the code fails to compile, because match must be exhaustive."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: forgetting a variant and having no _ arm, which is a compile error rather than a silent bug. Mistake: putting a broad _ arm before specific arms, which shadows them since the first matching arm wins. Exit: (a) Must a match cover every case? (b) What is the compact one-pattern alternative to match? Answers: yes, unless a wildcard covers the rest; if let."}]}'::jsonb, 'A match must be exhaustive: every possible value has to be covered, or you use _ as a catch-all wildcard arm.', 13),
  (11, 'Error handling with Result and Option', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Model absence with Option, recoverable failure with Result, and propagate errors cleanly with the ? operator. Warm-up: what does Rust use in place of a null value?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Rust has no null. A value that might be missing has type Option<T>, which is either Some(value) or None, so the compiler forces you to handle the empty case. A computation that might fail has type Result<T, E>, which is Ok(value) on success or Err(error) on failure; nothing is thrown, and you cannot ignore the error by accident. To pass an error up the call stack without a pile of match arms, append the ? operator to a Result or Option: if it is Ok or Some, ? unwraps the inner value and execution continues; if it is Err or None, ? returns it from the current function immediately. This keeps the happy path readable while still handling every failure."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Return Option for a lookup that may find nothing:\n\n    fn first_char(s: &str) -> Option<char> {\n        s.chars().next()\n    }\n    match first_char(\"hi\") {\n        Some(c) => println!(\"first is {}\", c), // h\n        None => println!(\"empty\"),\n    }\n\nReturn Result for an operation that may fail:\n\n    fn divide(a: f64, b: f64) -> Result<f64, String> {\n        if b == 0.0 {\n            Err(String::from(\"cannot divide by zero\"))\n        } else {\n            Ok(a / b)\n        }\n    }\n\nPropagate with ? so the happy path stays flat:\n\n    fn halve_then_report(a: f64) -> Result<f64, String> {\n        let result = divide(a, 2.0)?; // returns Err early if it fails\n        Ok(result + 1.0)\n    }"},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What are the two variants of Option? 2) What are the two variants of Result? 3) What does the ? operator do when applied to an Err value? 4) Why does Rust have no null? 5) What does ? do when applied to Ok(5)? Answers: 1) Some(value) and None. 2) Ok(value) and Err(error). 3) it returns that Err from the current function immediately. 4) to force the missing case to be handled at compile time via Option, eliminating null-pointer bugs. 5) it unwraps to 5 and execution continues."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: reaching for .unwrap() on every Result, which panics on failure instead of handling it; use match or ? in real code. Mistake: using ? in a function whose return type is not Result or Option, which will not compile. Exit: (a) Which type models a value that may be absent? (b) What does ? do on a Result that is Ok? Answers: Option<T>; it unwraps the contained value and continues."}]}'::jsonb, 'Option<T> models presence (Some) or absence (None); Result<T, E> models success (Ok) or failure (Err). The ? operator returns the error early and unwraps the success.', 15),
  (12, 'Generics and traits', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Write functions and types that work over many concrete types with generics, and constrain them with trait bounds. Warm-up: a trait is most like which idea from other languages?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Generics let you write a function or type once and use it with many concrete types, using a type parameter such as T. But a generic function can only do to a value what it knows the value supports. A trait defines a set of methods a type can implement, like a shared interface. A trait bound, written T: SomeTrait, restricts a generic so it accepts only types that implement that trait, which is what lets the body call those methods. You can also implement a trait for your own types to give them shared behavior. Generics compile to specialized code for each concrete type used, so there is no runtime cost."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"A generic function with a trait bound so it can compare values:\n\n    fn largest<T: PartialOrd + Copy>(list: &[T]) -> T {\n        let mut max = list[0];\n        for &item in list.iter() {\n            if item > max {\n                max = item;\n            }\n        }\n        max\n    }\n    let nums = [3, 7, 2, 9, 4];\n    println!(\"{}\", largest(&nums)); // 9\n\nDefining a trait and implementing it for a type:\n\n    trait Summary {\n        fn summarize(&self) -> String;\n    }\n    struct Article { title: String }\n    impl Summary for Article {\n        fn summarize(&self) -> String {\n            format!(\"Article: {}\", self.title)\n        }\n    }\n    let a = Article { title: String::from(\"Rust\") };\n    println!(\"{}\", a.summarize()); // Article: Rust"},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What does the type parameter T stand for in a generic function? 2) Why does `largest` need the bound T: PartialOrd? 3) What does a trait define? 4) How do you give your own struct a trait''s behavior? 5) Do generics cost extra at runtime? Answers: 1) a placeholder for whatever concrete type the caller uses. 2) so the body can use the > comparison, which PartialOrd provides. 3) a set of methods that implementing types must provide. 4) with an impl Trait for Type block. 5) no; generics are compiled to specialized code per type, so there is no runtime overhead."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: calling a method or operator in a generic body without a bound that guarantees it, which the compiler rejects. Mistake: confusing a trait (behavior) with a struct (data); a type has fields, a trait has methods it promises. Exit: (a) What does a trait bound like T: PartialOrd do? (b) Where do you write the code that makes a type implement a trait? Answers: it restricts T to types offering that behavior so the body can use it; in an impl Trait for Type block."}]}'::jsonb, 'Generics like <T> write code once for many types; a trait bound like T: PartialOrd restricts T to types that provide the needed behavior.', 15),
  (13, 'Checkpoint 3', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"A match expression must be...","options":["sorted","exhaustive, covering every case","limited to two arms","free of wildcards"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The compiler requires that every possible value is handled, or a _ arm covers the rest."},{"prompt":"The idiomatic replacement for a null value in Rust is...","options":["Result","Option with Some and None","an empty String","the () unit type"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Option<T> forces the absent case to be handled, eliminating null-pointer bugs."},{"prompt":"Applied to an Err value, the ? operator...","options":["panics immediately","returns the Err from the current function","converts it to Ok","ignores it"],"answer":1,"explanation":"? propagates the error by returning it early, keeping the success path flat."},{"prompt":"A trait bound such as T: PartialOrd exists so that...","options":["T runs faster","the generic body may use behavior that trait guarantees","T becomes a reference","the function returns a Result"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The bound restricts T to types that provide the needed methods, here comparison."},{"prompt":"The if let construct is best described as...","options":["a loop","a compact match for a single pattern of interest","an error type","a trait"],"answer":1,"explanation":"if let handles one pattern concisely and ignores the rest."},{"prompt":"Generics in Rust incur...","options":["a garbage collection pause","no runtime overhead, via per-type specialization","boxing of every value","a dynamic dispatch cost always"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The compiler generates specialized code for each concrete type, so generics are zero-cost."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (14, 'Collections and iterators', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Build and grow a vector, look values up in a hash map, and transform data with iterator adapters. Warm-up: does calling .map() on an iterator do any work by itself?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Vec<T> is Rust''s growable list: you push elements onto it and index into it. HashMap<K, V> stores key-to-value pairs for fast lookup. Iterators tie the collections to a clean, composable style of processing. Calling .iter() gives an iterator, and adapters like map (transform each element) and filter (keep some elements) build a new iterator without doing any work yet, because iterators are lazy. Work happens only when a consumer runs the chain: collect gathers results into a collection, sum adds them, and a for loop walks them. This lets you express a data pipeline as a readable chain that compiles to a tight loop."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"A growable vector:\n\n    let mut v: Vec<i32> = Vec::new();\n    v.push(10);\n    v.push(20);\n    println!(\"{} {}\", v.len(), v[0]); // 2 10\n\nA hash map lookup:\n\n    use std::collections::HashMap;\n    let mut scores = HashMap::new();\n    scores.insert(\"alice\", 90);\n    scores.insert(\"bob\", 75);\n    if let Some(s) = scores.get(\"alice\") {\n        println!(\"alice: {}\", s); // alice: 90\n    }\n\nAn iterator pipeline: keep the even numbers, double them, sum:\n\n    let nums = vec![1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6];\n    let total: i32 = nums.iter()\n        .filter(|&&n| n % 2 == 0)  // 2, 4, 6\n        .map(|&n| n * 2)           // 4, 8, 12\n        .sum();                     // 24\n    println!(\"{}\", total);"},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Which type is a growable list? 2) How do you add an element to a vector? 3) What does .get on a HashMap return, given a key may be missing? 4) Why is .map() called lazy? 5) What consumer would turn an iterator into a Vec? Answers: 1) Vec<T>. 2) call .push(value) on a mutable vector. 3) an Option, Some(value) if present or None if absent. 4) it builds a new iterator and does no work until a consumer drives it. 5) collect, as in .collect::<Vec<_>>()."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: building a map/filter chain and expecting it to run without a consumer - nothing happens until collect, sum, or a for loop. Mistake: indexing a vector out of bounds, which panics; prefer .get for a safe Option. Exit: (a) What makes iterator adapters lazy? (b) Which consumer sums an iterator of numbers? Answers: they produce a new iterator and defer work until consumed; sum."}]}'::jsonb, 'Vec<T> is a growable list; iterator adapters like map and filter are lazy and do nothing until a consumer like collect or sum drives them.', 14),
  (15, 'Capstone: a word-frequency counter', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Read a complete small Rust program that counts how often each word appears in a text, and see every earlier concept working together. Warm-up: which collection would you use to map each word to a count?"},{"heading":"The program","text":"The goal: given a sentence, report how many times each word occurs. We borrow the text as &str, split it into word slices, and tally counts in a HashMap.\n\n    use std::collections::HashMap;\n\n    fn word_counts(text: &str) -> HashMap<String, u32> {\n        let mut counts: HashMap<String, u32> = HashMap::new();\n        for word in text.split_whitespace() {\n            let counter = counts.entry(word.to_string()).or_insert(0);\n            *counter += 1;\n        }\n        counts\n    }\n\n    fn main() {\n        let text = \"the cat sat on the mat the cat\";\n        let counts = word_counts(text);\n        match counts.get(\"the\") {\n            Some(n) => println!(\"the: {}\", n), // the: 3\n            None => println!(\"the: 0\"),\n        }\n        println!(\"cat: {}\", counts.get(\"cat\").copied().unwrap_or(0)); // cat: 2\n    }"},{"heading":"How it works, line by line","text":"word_counts takes text by shared reference &str, so it borrows and does not take ownership. split_whitespace returns an iterator of &str word slices, each borrowing part of the input rather than copying it. For each word, entry(word.to_string()).or_insert(0) either finds the existing entry or inserts a fresh 0, and returns a &mut u32 to it; we then write through that mutable reference with *counter += 1. The map is returned by value, moving ownership to the caller. In main, counts.get returns an Option, which we handle with match, and the second lookup uses copied().unwrap_or(0) to default a missing word to 0."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Why does word_counts take &str instead of String? 2) What does split_whitespace produce? 3) What does entry(...).or_insert(0) return? 4) Why must counter be dereferenced with * before += 1? 5) What does get return, and how is the missing case handled here? Answers: 1) to borrow the text without taking ownership, and to accept both String values and literals. 2) an iterator of &str word slices that borrow the input. 3) a mutable reference to the count for that key, creating it as 0 if absent. 4) counter is a &mut u32, so * reaches the integer it points to. 5) an Option; match handles Some/None, and unwrap_or(0) supplies a default of 0."},{"heading":"Where to go next and exit ticket","text":"Extensions to try: lowercase each word so ''The'' and ''the'' merge; return the most frequent word by iterating the map and tracking a max; sort the pairs before printing. Each reuses skills from this course. Exit: (a) Which concept lets word_counts read the text without copying it? (b) Which collection maps each word to its count? Answers: borrowing via a shared &str reference; HashMap<String, u32>."}]}'::jsonb, 'This program ties together ownership, borrowing, slices, HashMap, iterators, Option, and pattern matching in one small, compilable whole.', 16),
  (16, 'Final quiz', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Rust guarantees memory safety without a garbage collector by...","options":["scanning the heap periodically","enforcing ownership and borrowing at compile time","reference counting every value","forbidding the heap entirely"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The compiler proves memory is used correctly through ownership and borrowing, so there is no runtime collector."},{"prompt":"After a String is moved into a new binding, the original binding is...","options":["a copy","no longer usable","set to null","shared"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A move transfers ownership for non-Copy types and invalidates the source."},{"prompt":"At any one moment a value may have...","options":["one mutable reference or many shared references, not both","many mutable references","one shared and one mutable reference","no references ever"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The borrow checker allows either a single mutable reference or any number of shared references."},{"prompt":"A &str slice is cheap because it stores...","options":["a deep copy of the text","only a pointer and a length","the whole owning String","an index into a global table"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A slice borrows a range as a pointer plus length and copies no character data."},{"prompt":"A value that might be absent should have the type...","options":["Result<T, E>","Option<T>","Vec<T>","&T"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Option<T> models Some(value) or None and forces the empty case to be handled."},{"prompt":"Applied to a Result that is Ok, the ? operator...","options":["returns early","unwraps the value and continues","panics","converts to None"],"answer":1,"explanation":"On Ok it unwraps the inner value; only on Err does it return early."},{"prompt":"A trait bound like T: PartialOrd on a generic function lets the body...","options":["allocate on the heap","use the comparison behavior that trait provides","skip type checking","return a trait object"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The bound restricts T to types offering that behavior, so the body may call it."},{"prompt":"Iterator adapters such as map and filter do their work...","options":["immediately when called","only when a consumer like collect or sum drives the chain","on a background thread","never"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Iterators are lazy; adapters build a new iterator and defer work until a consumer runs it."},{"prompt":"A match expression that omits a possible variant and has no wildcard will...","options":["run the first arm","fail to compile","panic at runtime","return None"],"answer":1,"explanation":"match must be exhaustive, so a missing case with no _ arm is a compile error."},{"prompt":"The growable list type in Rust''s standard library is...","options":["Array<T>","Vec<T>","List<T>","Slice<T>"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Vec<T> is a heap-allocated, growable list you push onto and index into."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10)
) as v(pos, title, kind, body, notes, mins)
